--
The frontend now produces a real body for `m[1] = 42;`, so BMC explores the
fully-inlined libstdc++ std::map::operator[] machinery.  Unlike set::insert
(which takes the key directly and verifies in ~0.04s of symex), operator[]
builds a std::pair<const Key, T> by piecewise construction (std::tuple /
forward_as_tuple / piecewise_construct) and performs a lower_bound search plus
node insertion -- all inline header code.  Unwinding that to the depth needed
for correctness makes the SAT formula grow super-exponentially in the unwind
bound: roughly 0.2M vars / 0.2M clauses at --unwind 3, 1.7M / 2.0M at 4, and
19.8M / 57.2M (with 81k VCCs, ~24s symex + ~25s SSA conversion) at 5, after
which the solver runs out of resources.  Disabling pointer/bounds checks does
not help: the equation size comes from the unwound code itself, not the
checks.

This is a bounded-model-checking scaling limit on the inlined map machinery,
independent of the std::_Rb_tree_* compiled-library models CBMC now provides
(those cover the out-of-line functions; operator[]'s heavy machinery is all
header/inline code).  Addressing it would need a lighter model/stub for the
tuple/piecewise pair construction rather than solver tuning.  Kept as
KNOWNBUG; std::set and std::map find/count/insert(node)/iteration/erase verify
quickly.
